<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ahmadnagar Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1490 CE – 1636 CE · capital Ahmadnagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Nizam Shahi sultanate of the northern Deccan, remembered for Chand Bibi's defence of Ahmadnagar and Malik Ambar's resistance to the Mughals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-nizam-shah-i">Malik Ahmad Nizam Shah</a>, son of a Bahmani minister, defeated a Bahmani army in 1490 and declared independence. In 1494 he founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> as his capital, establishing the Nizam Shahi dynasty in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Nizam Shahs annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/berar-sultanate">Berar Sultanate</a> in 1574 and took part in the victory over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Talikota</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-bibi">Chand Bibi</a>, acting as regent, defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> against Mughal attack in 1595. After the capital fell, the Ethiopian-born minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a> kept the state alive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Khadki</a> (later Aurangabad), pioneering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare and a land revenue survey later adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in 1600, but resistance continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a> and his son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji Bhonsle</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> general, propped up a puppet Nizam Shah until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> forced his surrender in 1636 and annexed the remaining territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ahmadnagar-sultanate.jpg" alt="Ahmadnagar Sultanate" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mr.Hanes · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmednagar_Sultanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1490 CE – 1636 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ahmadnagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Malik Ahmad Nizam Shah, Chand Bibi, Malik Ambar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Marathi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nizam Shahi dynasty, Nizam Shahis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1563&amp;focus=ahmadnagar-sultanate" aria-label="Ahmadnagar Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6701 1470 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ahmadnagar Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7021 1726L7088 1790M7021 1726L7018 1385M7021 1726L7083 1648"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1790h0M7018 1385h0M7083 1648h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7021 1726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7021 1726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1563&amp;focus=ahmadnagar-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
